STEP 4 — the remote-id is configurable, and reverts  (S1566.100.02, .07, .08)
================================================================================
  awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-relay agent-option remote-id CK-REMOTE-82   (accepted now)
  show ip dhcp-relay -> The Remote Id string for Relay Agent Option is CK-REMOTE-82

  leg `remote-id-ascii`, 3 clients. COUNTED: 6 relayed, offers 3/3, acks 3/3.
  SERVER side, identical on all three:
      raw = 0106 766c616e3130 020e 010c 434b2d52454d4f54452d3832
      sub-option 1 (circuit-id) = "vlan10"          <- UNCHANGED, as .06 requires
      sub-option 2 (remote-id)  = 01 0c "CK-REMOTE-82"   (type 1 = ASCII, length 12)

  Then the revert (S1566.100.08):
  awplus(config-if)# no ip dhcp-relay agent-option remote-id
  show ip dhcp-relay -> The Remote Id string for Relay Agent Option is e01a.ead4.7043
  and the next leg's frames carried `0208 0006e01aead47043` again — back to the switch MAC on
  the wire, not merely in the show output.
STEP 4 PASS. Note the inner encoding changes with the content: type 0x00 + 6-byte MAC for the
default, type 0x01 + length for an ASCII string.

================================================================================
STEP 5 — `agent-option checking` validates SERVER RESPONSES, not client ingress
================================================================================
⚠ My triage plan had this wrong, and the documentation corrected it before any verdict was
written. I had planned to test `checking` as an untrusted-ingress drop. It is not that. The
AW+ command reference is explicit:

  "This command enables the DHCP Relay Agent to check DHCP Relay Agent Information Option
   (Option 82) information in RESPONSE PACKETS RETURNED FROM DHCP SERVERS. If the information
   does not match the information it has for its own client (downstream) interface then the
   DHCP Relay Agent drops the packet."

Client-originated option 82 is governed by a DIFFERENT command,
`ip dhcp-relay information policy` — tested as STEP 6.

The wrong-direction leg is recorded because it is the evidence for the correction:
  leg `checking-on-with-opt82`: checking ENABLED, client injects its own option 82
    -> 6 relayed, offers 3/3. NOT dropped — correct, because `checking` does not police ingress.

The correct matched pair, both with `ip dhcp-relay agent-option checking` ENABLED, differing
only in what the SERVER returns:
  (a) server ECHOES the relay's own option 82 (dhcp4.py server --echo-option82):
        server sent 3 OFFERs -> **3 reached the client** (offers 3/3)
  (b) server returns a MISMATCHED option 82
        (dhcp4.py server --option82-override circuit:WRONGVLAN,remote:WRONGREMOTE):
        server sent 3 OFFERs -> **0 reached the client** (offers 0/3, 0 Replies on eth3)

The relay dropped every response whose option 82 did not match what it had for its own
downstream interface, and passed every response that matched. STEP 5 PASS, in the direction the
feature actually works.

================================================================================
STEP 6 — `ip dhcp-relay information policy`: all four actions on client option 82
================================================================================
The guide, p21-22: the policy "sets the action that the DHCP relay should take when a received
DHCP client request contains Option 82 information" — keep / append / drop / replace (default).
Each leg sends the SAME crafted DISCOVER carrying option 82
(circuit-id "CKCKT99", remote-id "CKREM99"); only the policy differs. Checking was disabled for
these legs so the response path could not confound the ingress measurement.

  | policy             | relayed | option 82 arriving at the server                       |
  | ------------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
  | replace (default)  |   3/3   | ONE TLV = the DUT's own: "vlan10" / MAC                |
  | keep               |   3/3   | ONE TLV = the client's, untouched: "CKCKT99"/"CKREM99" |
  | append             |   3/3   | TWO TLVs: the client's FIRST, then the DUT's own       |
  | drop               |  **0**  | nothing relayed, offers 0/3                            |

`drop` is the control that must not match, and it produced exactly zero.

**The `append` result nearly became a false defect, and this is how it was caught.** The leg
summary first showed only the client's "CKCKT99"/"CKREM99" and no DUT field, which reads as
"append did not append". That was MY tool: `dhcp4.py`'s option dictionary kept only the FIRST
option of a given name, and an appended option 82 is a SECOND TLV with the same name. Dumping
the raw frame (work/append-check.py) settled it:

    policy-append, chaddr 02e80000a001 — option-82 TLVs present: 2
      [0] len=18 raw=0107434b434b5439390207434b52454d3939
            sub 1 len 7 = b'CKCKT99'      sub 2 len 7 = b'CKREM99'
      [1] len=18 raw=0106766c616e313002080006e01aead47043
            sub 1 len 6 = b'vlan10'       sub 2 len 8 = b'\\x00\\x06\\xe0\\x1a\\xea\\xd4pC'
    policy-keep,   chaddr 02e800008001 — option-82 TLVs present: 1  (the client's only)
    replace,       chaddr 02e800004001 — option-82 TLVs present: 1  (the DUT's only)

`append` appends correctly: the client's field is preserved and the relay's own is added after
it, which is the documented behaviour for a trusted DHCP-snooping switch upstream. dhcp4.py now
reports `option82_count` and `option82_all` so a duplicate can never hide again.
STEP 6 PASS on all four actions.

================================================================================
STEP 7 — option 82 is STRIPPED from the reply delivered to the client (RFC 3046)
================================================================================
Measured in EVERY leg above, on the client side, and it is a real test here because the scapy
server was deliberately run with `--echo-option82`: each OFFER arriving at the relay from the
server CARRIED option 82. In all six insertion legs the OFFER that reached the client on eth3
carried none:
      02:e8:00:00:20:01 -> OFFER 192.168.10.100  option82: ABSENT (stripped)
      ... identically for every client in every leg
The relay removes the agent information before handing the reply down to the client, as
RFC 3046 requires. STEP 7 PASS.

